  "prompt_text": "This figure is a landscape cross-section showing how thawing permafrost across northern ecosystems releases greenhouse gases. It links surface environments—boreal forest, wetlands, tundra, streams, thermokarst lakes, and burned areas—to subsurface layers including organic soil, mineral soil, continuous and discontinuous permafrost, and deeper Yedoma deposits.\n\nThe main message is that different thaw processes expose frozen carbon in different ways and produce different gas emissions. Blue arrows indicate methane (CH4) and red arrows indicate carbon dioxide (CO2). Gradual thaw beneath soils appears to release mainly CO2, while wetter features such as thermokarst lakes, wetlands, and saturated tundra emit substantial CH4, often along with CO2. Abrupt thaw, shown by ground collapse and lake formation, seems to create especially strong emissions because deeper frozen material becomes available. Fire in the boreal forest is also depicted as a direct source of CO2 and as a disturbance that can promote thaw.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that permafrost carbon is stored at depth across a heterogeneous landscape, and that warming, wetting, and disturbance shift this carbon into the atmosphere through both methane and carbon dioxide, with emission type depending strongly on local conditions.",
